    heading: 'How crab is priced at Kep Crab Market',
    body: `<p>Crab at the market is sold <strong>by weight</strong>, usually priced per kilogram and weighed on the stall's own scale in front of you. You are generally choosing between <strong>live crab</strong> (kept in baskets or ice boxes) and crab that a nearby restaurant will cook for you. Live, unprocessed crab costs less than a plated dish, because the cooking, sides and service are added on top.</p>
    <p>Two things move the price most: <strong>size</strong> and <strong>species</strong>. Larger crabs and the prized blue swimmer crab (sometimes called Kep crab) tend to cost more per kilogram than smaller or commoner kinds. Prices also shift with the season and with how good the catch has been that week.</p>`,
  },
  {
    heading: 'A realistic way to budget',
    body: `<p>Rather than a fixed menu, think of it as two steps: the <strong>crab itself</strong> (weighed and priced by the kg) plus a <strong>cooking and service charge</strong> if you eat at one of the market restaurants. A sensible approach is to agree the total — crab weight, price, and cooking fee — <strong>before</strong> it goes on the grill, and to confirm whether pepper sauce, sides and rice are included.</p>
    <p>If you only want to take crab home, buy it live and have it cleaned; you then skip the restaurant markup entirely. Many travellers compare the per-kg rate at two or three stalls before deciding.</p>`,
